 -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186319174 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186319174 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186319174 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186321061 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186321061 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186321065 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186321065 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186321065 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186321065 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186348203 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186348203 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186410545 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=2$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186410545 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534647 17592186045417 false] [17592186430809 1080 "II only" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430809 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430809 1083 #uuid "249765f6-42b9-5e83-a75a-2fb40b35fb7f" 131941395