ndent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430827 1072 #uuid "48a8881c-e6ac-54a2-9375-24bf5efbb3a5" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430827 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So,  ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430825 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430828 1069 "q-35300" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430828 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430828 1072 #uuid "6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430828 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430824 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430829 1069 "q-79321" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430829 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430829 1072 #uuid "fd38fa95-000b-54ff-9226-2d221d8be579" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true] [17592186430829 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$" 13194139534647 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-249765f6-42b9-5e83-a75a-2fb40b35fb7f" 17592186430809 "answer-5e5d85dd-4d17-573a-8b74-42d7cabad2c2" 17592186430810 "answer-6c7cbc7a-dd9b-537a-83c0-ac5cd300c350" 17592186430811 "answer-86142c8b-886b-5e6e-9f79-4adb8275d8d7" 17592186430812 "answer-8fb95b63-4d2c-5d43-bd50-a2fba6e30b2f" 17592186430813 "answer-9cee02f8-68cf-5851-8f1a-97dfedd2201f" 17592186430814 "answer-9eeeff2c-a26a-5efe-999b-770dee902f46" 17592186430815 "answer-9ff06e75-fb08-5fe4-bd16-8b0cce240878" 17592186430816 "answer-a027a479-344f-5843-8902-bf22751ea32f" 17592186430817 "answer-ae48cc0a-d8a5-5c9e-8d7b-c02523c38386" 17592186430818 "answer-bd58722b-d78b-5b4d-ac72-d93c0e1f07e9" 17592186430819 "answer-c7d9e5de-adc7-5e5d-8e0c-0e55b15b1b69" 17592186430820 "answer-e1e20a6a-14b7-54fa-9105-33f5512ce16c" 17592186430821 "answer-e682f472-fa87-510a-b1cc-a0ffe0cfc749" 17592186430822 "answer-f15db031-2bc3-5368-8dd0-db33cdf3c7fe" 17592186430823 "answer-field-1068104a-c084-560a-bbeb-c3ccd952752a" 17592186430824 "answer-field-a08b0b02-a55d-50fe-a6cc-f8c71f19c1af" 17592186430825 "answer-field-c1c029bf-4874-5367-aefd-b2eea5af8718" 17592186430826 "question-48a8881c-e6ac-54a2-9375-24bf5efbb3a5" 17592186430827 "question-6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" 17592186430828 "question-fd38fa95-000b-54ff-9226-2d221d8be579" 17592186430829}}
